A||N||21||stimpy||Hey Cliff, a little help for the WebTVers in the crowd?||||5||
Z||000000||stimpy||11-10-2000||11:28 PM||||Hey Cliff, whenever I go to read a lengthy thread, by the time it hits about 30 post, I get a message stating that the page is to big to load. It gets kinda frustrating. I was wondering if there is any type of control you may have over the number of posts that can fit on each page of a thread. It seems that up to 25 posts it is clear sailing.  <BR>  I know that I am part of a minority that uses WebTV, but if it is possible to change this, I'm sure my fellow Web TVers like AL, philphine and Xtreme Fieros would appreciate it as much as I.  Thanks in advance.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||11-11-2000||02:15 AM||||stimpy, i gave up the messenger service web tv has and that helped a lot. still don't see threads with a lot of pics (unless i let it spool up while i go get a sandwich or some thing), but i see whole threads most of the time.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||11-11-2000||06:31 AM||||Stimpy, I need to know first what others think about it. The reason I chose 40 messages per page is because at first people didn't like the thought of spreading messages over several pages at all. But if nobody minds, I can change it to 25 mpp.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-11-2000||12:32 PM||||I'm likely to take a bunch of heat for this... Oh well. This year reeks already.<P>The more the pages start breaking up into additional pages the bigger a pain in the ____ the whole site becomes to read. The thing that needs to be looked at is the number of pages this would affect. We hit 25 items in a thread real quick these days. With the annoyingly large size of a number of people's signature tags it's making for some real huge threads fast. (Any sig bigger than 1/2 – 3/4 vertical inches is really annoying. Especially when it appears over and over.)<P>I hate to alienate a group of users but I don't see why the rest of us should be inconvenienced by yet another poorly thought out and supported Microsoft product. (Microsoft’s only real interest in this product was that it was a viable competitor. Like all Microsoft competitors it had to be bought stolen or destroyed as quickly as possible.) <P>Yes I use Win9x and IE, No I don’t like them, but that’s what I’m stuck with unless I can afford a new Mac or take several months to learn a Linux derivative and all the applications to do my work. I’m so tired of rebooting the machine because of IE memory bleeds. Office 2000 has bloated all my web pages so bad it’s not funny but I just don’t have time to do it by hand anymore. I love how Microsoft just assumes I want style sheets in everything. (Netscape isn’t any better these days. AOL has bloated the hell out of it too. Used to be a nice browser once upon a time. Now it’s just a flaky and full of security problems as IE.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||11-11-2000||12:39 PM||||Is the solution a routine that would automatically delete signatures from threads that exceed 30 posts ?<P>If it is, I'm sure no one would mind...after all, this service is free...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||11-11-2000||01:49 PM||||Ogre, the latest versions of some Linux distributions (like RedHat) have a real simple installation program, they're almost as easy as a Win installation. Installating Netscape on Linux is a breeze also.<P>My system is dual-boot. I'm usually in Win but that's simply because I like playing online games.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Linux isn't really that hard to learn, once you understand the idea behind it. My advantage was that I used to do a lot of programming for Unix systems. Another advantage is that most Linux apps are freeware. Even StarOffice (which is similar to Microsoft Office) is free. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||11-11-2000||03:00 PM||||I don't think that the <B>length</B> of the posts are the issue, or the use of signatures.  I just think that it is the number of post that screw up WebTV. As far as the benefit to non-WebTVers, as has been mentioned, many threads exceed 30 posts quite rapidly. I know I don't always want to scroll through 30 odd messages just to read what was added recently. For example, does anyone read the first two or three pages of the air freshener thread every time just to see whats been added last?  Talk about a pain in the ayass!  I think that in addition to helping out this "small group of users" who are actually pretty active in this forum, it would make it more convenient for the rest of the users to keep up with lenghty threads. My 2 pfennings.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-11-2000||08:03 PM||||Hmmm..... following that logic about the air freshener thread.... if we caused more pages to be generated it would make more sense to also open on the last page instead of the first. That would reduce the annoyance factor of multipage threads allot. <P>It usually takes a couple days before a thread breaks over. Most people would be folowing it as it did. The one that don't come in often could easily enough go back and read the rest. (How many pages is that clutch thread now......)||||reg||1
